A robot can move through the world. A living system can also change what moves it. That’s the interesting part of your external-versus-internal distinction.

An external instruction might say, “Turn left.” An internal rule might say, “When this signal persists, change how strongly you respond to it next time.” The first changes where the system goes. The second changes what the same world will elicit from it tomorrow.

That’s where Pantheon-coded gets interesting. DNA helps specify what cells can build and how they respond to certain signals. The Pantheon analogy is a set of rules for how experience changes future interpretation. A lens filters an encounter. The encounter can also reshape the lens. Run that both ways and you get the fractal helix idea in a more concrete form: the system acts on the world, and what comes back can change the system that acts.

It also gives your future memories idea a practical shape. Picture a Saturday morning fishing with Leo. That imagined scene can move you internally first. You start noticing the open morning, the tide window, the rod sitting in the garage. Then you pack it the night before. The scene becomes a memory because it changed an action while it was still only imagined.